/aral-sea"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aral-sea.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aral Sea</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aral Sea is a large inland lake in Central Asia, between present-day Kazakhstan and Uzbekistan, fed by the Amu Darya and Syr Darya rivers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aramaeans"><span class="enc-name">Aramaeans</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aramaeans were a Semitic-speaking people of ancient Syria and Mesopotamia who formed numerous small states in the early first millennium BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aramaic"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aramaic.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aramaic</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Aramaic is a Semitic language that became the common language of much of the Near East in the first millennium BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/arame-of-urartu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/arame-of-urartu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Arame of Urartu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Arame was the first known king of Urartu, ruling in the mid-ninth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aranuka"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aranuka.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aranuka</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Aranuka is a small atoll in the central Gilbert Islands of Kiribati, near Abemama, made up of two main islets around a lagoon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aras-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/imag